// --- PY-L2-CROSS-RUNTIME-DRIFT: positive cross-runtime divergence receipt ---
// Complement of the L2 cross-target PARITY lane (PR #4154,
// `run_cross_target_mvp2_python_parity_transport`), which proves runtime-value EQUALITY on the
// common domain (R2a add/order, R3 symbol projection). This carrier proves the COMPLEMENT
// obligation named in the Python RCA worksheet (Worksheet C): the SAME modeled program — exact
// integer add at the fixed-width boundary — DIVERGES across runtimes. Python realizes Int as
// arbitrary precision (the correct sum); Rust `i64` / Go `int64` realize it as fixed-width
// two's-complement with defined wraparound. That divergence is a MODELED fact, not a bug: the
// receipt asserts it POSITIVELY, so the verification system demonstrably DETECTS drift instead of
// silently treating fixed-width wraparound as parity. The expected divergence is anchored to
// `ValueDiff<String>` (the host_run divergence carrier) — `expected` is the arbitrary-precision
// value, `actual` is the fixed-width wrapped value — so the receipt references a modeled value
// pair, never a script-local literal. Go is a corroborating fixed-width witness (rust == go);
// the runner asserts it only when the Go toolchain is present.
//
// FACTS FLOW FORWARD (INVARIANTS P1/P2): the drift is not asserted as a bare value pair — the
// MODELED CAUSE travels with it. The three `*_fact` fields anchor the divergence to the extdeps
// language representation facts that JUSTIFY it: Python int is `python_repr_int_arbitrary_precision`
// (so the sum is exact) while Rust and Go integers are `rust_repr_integer_twos_complement` /
// `go_repr_twos_complement_integer` (so the same add wraps at the fixed-width boundary). The
// `TestClaim` asserts these anchors, so the receipt carries the reason for the drift, not just its
// observed values — that is what makes this carrier reusable authority rather than a literal pair.
//
// 🟡 gated — feature:leaf-model-cross-runtime-drift — dissolve-on: the modeled `TestClaim` runner
// exercises `src/v2/test/claim/language_model/python_cross_runtime_drift.dag` directly (retiring the
// boundary host-process bridge `scripts/v2-leaf-model-python-cross-runtime-drift-verify.sh` +
// `v2_leaf_model_python_cross_runtime_drift_test.rs`, shrinking `EXPECTED_HAND_AUTHORED_TEST` by 1);
// forbidden: a parallel drift-probe vocabulary outside this carrier — extend it, don't fork it.
type LeafModelCrossRuntimeDriftProbe {
  claim_id: LeafModelClaimId
  // Modeled cause of the divergence (extdeps representation facts) — Facts Flow Forward.
  arbitrary_precision_fact: Symbol
  fixed_width_rust_fact: Symbol
  fixed_width_go_fact: Symbol
  arbitrary_precision_source: String
  fixed_width_rust_source: String
  fixed_width_go_source: String
  expected_divergence: ValueDiff<String>
}

// --- PY-L2-CROSS-TARGET-PARITY: positive cross-target behavioral PARITY receipt ---
// The exact COMPLEMENT of LeafModelCrossRuntimeDriftProbe. Drift proves the same modeled program
// DIVERGES at the fixed-width boundary; this proves the same modeled program AGREES on the common
// domain — the subset where arbitrary-precision and two's-complement realizations coincide (small
// integer add) and where external Symbol projection displays the same nominal payload (R3). This is
// the L2 release-minimum parity receipt named in the Python RCA Worksheet C: it asserts runtime-value
// EQUALITY across Python, Rust, and Go, so the verification system demonstrably DISTINGUISHES parity
// (common domain) from drift (boundary) rather than collapsing them.
//
// FACTS FLOW FORWARD (INVARIANTS P1/P2): parity is not a bare value equality — the MODELED CAUSE
// travels with it. The three `*_fact` fields anchor the agreement to the extdeps facts that JUSTIFY
// it: for R2a the integer representation facts (arbitrary-precision == two's-complement ON THE COMMON
// SUBDOMAIN), for R3 the atom-realization facts (same nominal Symbol payload). `expected_parity`
// reuses ValueDiff<String> as the host_run value pair (expected = Python payload, actual = Rust/Go
// payload); PARITY is the positive property that the two sides are EQUAL — the structural mirror of
// the drift probe's `expected != actual`. R2b arbitrary precision is intentionally NOT a parity row
// (it is the drift complement); order's boolean surface-formatting differs per language and is
// likewise excluded from the numeric parity payload.
//
// 🟡 gated — feature:leaf-model-cross-target-parity — dissolve-on: the modeled `TestClaim` runner
// executes the three target sources directly and binds the observed stdout to `expected_parity.actual`
// (today host execution is harness-blocked, same as the drift probe and the cross-runtime-drift
// runner); forbidden: a parallel parity vocabulary outside this carrier — extend it, don't fork it.
type LeafModelCrossTargetParityProbe {
  claim_id: LeafModelClaimId
  // Modeled cause of the agreement (extdeps facts) — Facts Flow Forward.
  python_fact: Symbol
  rust_fact: Symbol
  go_fact: Symbol
  python_source: String
  rust_source: String
  go_source: String
  expected_parity: ValueDiff<String>
}

